# PAM policy for Aspia operating system / domain accounts (RHEL/Fedora). Used by the host service
# to check the password of a client that signs in with "DOMAIN\user" or "user@domain". The computer
# must be joined to the domain (realmd + sssd, or winbind); system-auth then includes pam_sss.so or
# pam_winbind.so. Installed only if the file does not exist yet, so local changes survive upgrades.
# pam_unix waits about 2 seconds after a wrong password (and not after a correct one). The host does
# not rely on it: every failed logon is answered 4 seconds (or the next multiple of 4 seconds if the
# logon is slower) after the credentials are received, so the
# answer time does not reveal whether the password was correct. "nodelay" is therefore not needed in
# the system files included here.
#
# System accounts (root, daemons: uid < 1000) can never sign in through Aspia; their passwords are
# not even checked. Lower the value only if the domain uses smaller uids.
#
# pam_faillock: system-auth contains it when the authselect feature is enabled
# ("authselect enable-feature with-faillock", the default of the sssd profile on RHEL 8+); failed
# Aspia logons are then counted and locked out together with ssh and the console. It is not added
# here as well, because it would count every failure twice. The host limits failed logons per
# account and per client address itself (with growing delays) in any case.
auth    requisite pam_succeed_if.so uid >= 1000 quiet
auth    include   system-auth
account required  pam_succeed_if.so uid >= 1000 quiet
account include   system-auth
